      "## Redefining Early‑Stage Hiring with AI Co‑Founders\nThe session titled “Hiring When AI Is a Co‑Founder” will take place at TechCrunch Disrupt 2026. It features Josh Reeves, CEO and co‑founder of Gusto; Michelle Johnson, senior vice president at Insight Partners; and John Koelliker, CEO and co‑founder of Leland. The discussion focuses on how early‑stage startups can build teams where humans and AI agents collaborate without sacrificing speed, accountability, or culture. Disrupt 2026 runs October 13–15 at Moscone West in San Francisco, gathering more than 10,000 startups, investors, and tech decision‑makers.",
      "## How Gusto, Insight Partners, and Leland See the Shift\nFor an early‑stage company, each new hire represents a trade‑off among capability, cost, and speed. Engineering work, customer support, and operational tasks are increasingly delegated to systems that can complete multi‑step processes on their own. Michelle Johnson advises CEOs and CROs across North America and Europe on go‑to‑market strategy, revenue organization, and AI implementation. Before joining Insight, she helped scale Flock Safety from less than $1 million to $90 million in annual recurring revenue while leading early sales and revenue operations. Founders still need people who can make decisions, take responsibility, understand customers, and shape the company culture. The central challenge is determining where human judgment adds the most value and where an AI agent can perform the role instead.",
      "## Practical Tasks AI Can Take Over Today\nEngineering tasks, customer support, and operational work can increasingly be delegated to systems capable of completing multistep tasks.",
      "## What Founders Still Need to Own\n Human leaders are still required to interpret ambiguous market signals, negotiate partnerships, and build trust with stakeholders. The panel suggests that the most valuable human contributions lie in areas where judgment, creativity, and relational intelligence are essential—domains where AI currently lacks depth.",
      "## What to Watch at Disrupt 2026\nThe three speakers will continue the conversation with the broader Disrupt audience across the Expo Hall, Startup Battlefield 200, and numerous networking sessions.  The session is categorized under AI, Startups, and TechCrunch Disrupt, signaling its relevance to founders seeking practical guidance on the next wave of workforce evolution."
